  <h2>“Just enough” wasn’t enough</h2>
  <p>
    It was supposed to be a quick MVP for a job assignment. I got too interested in legal text and kept
    going until it was a product.<Cite id="clausecraft" />
  </p>

  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Generates a complete contract from a prompt or a guided questionnaire.</li>
    <li>Analyses each clause for risk.</li>
    <li>Reviews the whole contract for missing clauses and compliance issues.</li>
    <li>Inline editing with undo and redo, then export.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Decisions</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Privacy first.</strong> No accounts and no server-side storage: drafts live in the browser’s
    localStorage, and only the text being analysed goes to the model.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Model calls behind Edge Functions.</strong> Supabase Edge Functions call the Hugging Face Router,
    so no keys reach the client and models can be swapped. Each call walks a fallback chain: Llama 3.1 70B
    Instruct first, then Mistral 7B Instruct, Gemma 2 2B and Zephyr 7B. A free demo can’t promise that any one
    hosted model is up, so the chain trades some quality for an answer that still arrives. If every model
    fails, generation falls back to a template.<Cite id="clausecraft" />
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The model writes, rules score.</strong> The risk score is not the model’s opinion. It comes from
    fixed rules over the clause text, so re-analysing an unchanged clause always gives the same rating. The
    model only writes the one- or two-sentence insight next to it.
  </p>

  <h2>Evidence</h2>
  <p>A clause, worked through by hand with the app’s own rules:</p>
  <blockquote>
    “The Receiving Party may use the information for evaluation purposes, and its confidentiality obligations
    are perpetual.”
  </blockquote>
  <ul>
    <li>Base risk 0.2, plus 0.3 for <em>perpetual</em>, plus 0.1 for the permissive <em>may</em>: <strong>0.6, Medium risk</strong>.</li>
    <li>Hints attached: verify whether a finite term is acceptable, and confirm the confidentiality scope, duration and permitted disclosures.</li>
    <li>The whole-contract check asks the model for strict JSON at temperature 0.1: which of eight standard clauses are missing, concrete compliance issues, and the governing jurisdiction. A tolerant parser pulls the JSON out even when the model wraps it in prose.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What I’d do next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Make the rules read negation. Today “no unlimited liability” scores as risky as “unlimited liability”.</li>
    <li>Rate a small set of clauses with a lawyer and measure the score against them before trusting it.</li>
    <li>Show the exact words behind every rating, so a reader can check it in one glance.</li>
  </ul>
</Article>
